-- Records the new edit_website agent tool (master brief section 7/22) and
-- the editing/ module architecture finding (in-memory version manager,
-- not production-ready). Applied live via Supabase MCP on 2026-09-02; this
-- file makes that reproducible from a fresh database.
insert into public.capability_registry
  (capability_key, name, description, category, skill, agent_tool_name, package_or_module, department, connection, required_permission, read_write, tenant_scope, cost_profile, risk, verification_method, status, status_notes, external_blocker, last_verified_at, last_verified_by)
values
  ('capability:edit_website_agent_tool', 'edit_website: the first agent tool that can actually change a Stratxcel-built website, not just report status', 'Master brief section 7/22: WhatsApp/Admin Chat can now apply a real natural-language website edit, not just check status (check_website_status previously said this was "dashboard-only for now"). Extracted the live HTTP route''s classify/fetch/apply/write/audit sequence into a shared function, lib/websites/apply-tenant-website-edit.ts, so the route (app/api/platform/website-factory/[projectId]/edit) and the new edit_website tool (lib/agent-core/website-tools.ts) call exactly one implementation. Real security upgrade found and applied in the same change: the route''s risk gating was a small inline keyword list with no prompt-injection/secret-exfiltration guard at all; packages/websites-and-domains already has a more complete classifier for exactly this, classifyEditRequest (editing/classifier.ts), with a genuine security check ("ignore previous instructions", "reveal secrets", "api_key", "service_role", script/javascript: payloads) that the live route never used -- now both do. Confirmed classifyEditRequest is safe to reuse standalone even though the rest of that editing/ module is not production-ready (see capability:editing_module_in_memory_prototype) -- it is a pure function with no dependency on the module''s in-memory version manager. applyNaturalLanguageEdit + the real apply_site_project_version RPC remain the actual persistent write path, unchanged. High-risk edits still require explicit confirmation; unrecognized instructions still honestly make no change rather than fabricating success.', 'ecosystem', null, 'edit_website', 'lib/websites/apply-tenant-website-edit.ts, lib/agent-core/website-tools.ts, app/api/platform/website-factory/[projectId]/edit/route.ts', 'Engineering', null, 'agent:mutate:website', 'read_write', 'tenant', 'free', 'low_mutation', 'New apply-tenant-website-edit.test.ts (7 real assertions: security-block, needs-confirmation, not-found, write-failure, applied, and not-applied paths), full test:website-factory suite (26 files) passes, website-factory-security.test.ts passes unchanged, website-factory-route-entry.test.ts passes unchanged, full-repo tsc --noEmit clean, lint clean, real NODE_ENV=production next build exit 0', 'REAL_EXPOSED', 'Supported edit patterns remain narrow (visual/copy restyle, About page addition) -- matches applyNaturalLanguageEdit''s real current pattern set honestly, not overclaimed. Expanding the recognized pattern set is a separate, future content-engine improvement.', null, now(), 'claude_session_2026-09-02'),
  ('capability:editing_module_in_memory_prototype', 'packages/websites-and-domains/editing/ (WebsiteEditingEngine, classifier, planner, applier, version-manager) is real but architecturally not production-ready: its version manager is a pure in-memory Map', 'Section 6 capability audit. Found while building edit_website (capability:edit_website_agent_tool): a genuinely more sophisticated natural-language edit engine already exists (classifyEditRequest with a real security guard, a structured planner/applier operating on WebsiteSpecification, a WebsiteEditingEngine orchestrator with rollback/publish) but is called only by packages/websites-and-domains'' own qa/auto-fix.ts and smoke tests -- never a real app/ route. Root cause identified, not assumed: WebsiteVersionManager (editing/version-manager.ts) stores all version history in `private versionStorage: Map<string, VersionSnapshot[]>` -- pure process memory, architecturally incompatible with a real serverless Next.js route (state does not survive across invocations/instances, and would be silently lost on any restart even in a persistent process). This is why it was never wired to production, not an oversight -- the live route correctly uses the simpler applyNaturalLanguageEdit + a real Postgres RPC (apply_site_project_version) for actual persistence instead. classifyEditRequest itself (the one genuinely reusable, dependency-free piece) has already been extracted into the live path -- see capability:edit_website_agent_tool. Making the rest of this module production-safe would require rewriting WebsiteVersionManager to persist to real tables, a real, separate, non-trivial piece of work.', 'ecosystem', null, null, 'packages/websites-and-domains/src/editing/version-manager.ts', 'Engineering', null, null, 'read', 'platform_only', 'free', 'read', 'Read editing/version-manager.ts directly: versionStorage is a private in-memory Map with no database read/write anywhere in the file. Confirmed via grep that WebsiteEditingEngine/websiteEditingEngine have zero real app/ callers.', 'REAL_NOT_EXPOSED', 'Not wired for a real, verified architectural reason (in-memory-only persistence), not neglect. Its one safely-reusable piece (classifyEditRequest) is already reused live. Rewriting the version manager to persist to real tables is a distinct, separate future task.', null, now(), 'claude_session_2026-09-02')
on conflict (capability_key) do nothing;
